**Theorem 5.2 (Application of the cubic criterion with full complementarity).**
Suppose the data (5.1) satisfy all conditions in (3.5), including
the global interior cubic inequality. Then

$$
e(T)\ge\beta-\tau^Ty\qquad(T\in\mathfrak C_1).
\tag{5.2}
$$

For any one feasible $x=\sum_fw_fk(a_f,b_f)\in\mathcal F$, with
$w_f>0$ and unit real independent factors, and any $z$ satisfying
all fifteen inequalities $-zI\preceq H_J(x)\preceq zI$,
equality $z=\beta-\tau^Ty$ holds exactly when the following
complementarity conditions hold:

$$
\begin{gathered}
\operatorname{ran}A_J^+\subseteq\ker(zI-H_J(x)),\qquad
\operatorname{ran}A_J^-\subseteq\ker(zI+H_J(x))
\quad(J\in\mathcal J_{15}),\\
\mathcal B_{C,y,\delta}(a_f)b_f=0\quad\text{for every }f.
\end{gathered}
\tag{5.3}
$$

When equality holds, after the exact normal form (2.2), a positive-weight atom with
canonical preparation $a(p)$ has its radial measurement in
$\ker\mathcal R(p)$, or its tangent measurement in
$\ker\mathcal T(p)$. A nonzero radial kernel vector requires
$\mathscr F(p)=0$, and a nonzero tangent kernel vector requires
$B_+(p)B_-(p)-t^2p_+p_-=0$. These requirements apply also to
boundary populations; determinant zero alone is not a kernel-vector
or TP certificate.

Proof. Theorem 3.2 makes every $\mathcal B(a)$ PSD. Use the
following credited slack identity of QJM Convention 6.3:

$$
\begin{aligned}
z-(\beta-\tau^Ty)
={}&\sum_J\operatorname{tr}\bigl[
A_J^+(zI-H_J(x))+A_J^-(zI+H_J(x))\bigr]\\
&+\sum_fw_f b_f^T\mathcal B_{C,y,\delta}(a_f)b_f.
\end{aligned}
\tag{5.4}
$$

Its use retains all three TP multipliers. To fix explicitly the
normalization and adjoint orientation, put
$L(x)=\sum_J\operatorname{tr}(Z_JS_x^\dagger(\Pi_J))$.
Equation (1.8) gives
$L(x)=\langle C,M\rangle+\sum_\nu\delta_\nu\eta_\nu$.
The first sum in (5.4) is $z-\beta+L(x)$.
Since the preparation factors are unit,
$\tau^Th(a_f)=1$, so

$$
\sum_fw_fh(b_f)=M^T\tau=\tau.
\tag{5.5}
$$

Equation (3.6) therefore makes the second sum exactly
$\tau^Ty-L(x)$, proving (5.4) for this same channel. This is an
application of the supplied slack identity, not an independent
general conic-duality assertion.

All summands are nonnegative PSD pairings. For $x\in\mathcal F$,
take $z=e(T_x)$ in (1.11); (5.4) proves
$e(T_x)\ge\beta-\tau^Ty$. For any original complex competitor,
Definition 1.5 supplies a possibly different channel $T_x$ with
$e(T)\ge e(T_x)$, giving (5.2). Thus the real-product test is
sufficient for the entire original complex class, rather than an
assumption that the original competitor already has real factors.

For PSD matrices $P,Q$, $\operatorname{tr}(PQ)=0$ exactly when
$\operatorname{ran}P\subseteq\ker Q$: the PSD matrix
$Q^{1/2}PQ^{1/2}$ then has trace zero and is zero, equivalently
$P^{1/2}Q^{1/2}=0$. Likewise a PSD matrix has zero quadratic form
at $b$ exactly when it annihilates $b$. Apply these facts term by
term to (5.4), whose atom weights are positive. This gives precisely
(5.3) in both directions.

Theorem 2.2 can be applied to this same $x$. Equivariance of
$\mathcal B$ transports kernels under the canonical-preparation
rotation. At a canonical preparation, (3.8) splits the quadratic
form into two nonnegative block quadratic forms. If an unsplit
measurement has zero form, each nonzero radial or tangent part
separately has zero form and hence lies in the corresponding kernel.
Alternatively apply (5.4) to its split generator decomposition.
This proves the asserted support conditions without assuming any
orbit support beforehand. $\square$

